  "body": "## Summary\n\nA reader who wants to understand what Hive Mind OS does currently has to assemble it from the README, eight role contracts in `roles.py`, the policy table, and the graph rules in `objectives.py`. This adds one page that shows the mechanism, plus a runnable example so the page's claims can be re-derived rather than trusted.\n\n**`docs/VISUAL_OVERVIEW.html`** — a self-contained page (no build step, no external scripts, works offline, light and dark themes). Four hand-authored SVG figures and colour-coded role cards cover:\n\n- **The gap** — one base commit cloned three times, with the Curator's acceptance checks sealed against the base *before* the Builder's candidate is handed over, and the two outcomes (bundle published / nothing published).\n- **A real run** — the 97-event ledger from `hive-mind demo`, every bar coloured by acting role, milestones marked at the seal (53), the handover (66), and the verdict (94). Alongside it: what one receipt holds, what the bundle contains, and the budget actually drawn.\n- **The eight roles** — each with its mission, declared capabilities, and quality gates, colour-coded by role and visually separating the three that execute repository work today from the five that are chartered and policy-enforced only.\n- **Working unsupervised** — how a role picks up work with no dispatcher: the ready set, the token-bound lease, and the five refusal paths (`BLOCKED_DEPENDENCY`, expired lease, policy refusal, `RETRYABLE_FAILED`, `WAITING_HUMAN`), with the six autonomy levels and the four non-delegable actions.\n- **The sample DAG** — nine nodes across six waves, the per-node schedule table, and the messages the validator returns for malformed graphs.\n- **Seven separable entry points** — `verify`, `demo`/`deliver`, `audit`, `kernel plan`/`graph`, `autopilot`, `enqueue`/`serve`/`resume`, and `benchmark`/`experiment`, each with the problem it solves alone and the command for it.\n- **Status** — a table that keeps the README's stated limits intact: `--backend model` not ready, Git local-only, five roles chartered but not executing, hostile-code isolation not claimed, no production use.\n\n**`examples/sample-work-dag/`** — builds that same graph with `OrchestratorPlanner` and `ObjectiveGraph`, then prints the topological order, the ready waves, the canonical digests, and the validator's own message for eight malformed variants. The digests quoted in the document are this script's output, so a rule change in `objectives.py` or `planner.py` shows up as a diff rather than as stale prose.\n\nNo file under `src/`, `.github/`, `evidence/`, `docs/architecture/`, or any schema is touched.\n\n## Governance tier\n\n- [x] Governance-lite: documentation-only correction with no behavior, product-claim, acceptance-criterion, security, architecture, policy, courtroom, or schema change.\n- [ ] Governed: all other changes.\n\nFour added files, all documentation: one HTML page under `docs/`, and one worked example under `examples/` alongside the existing `verify-an-agent-change`. Nothing importable by the package changes, and no existing file is modified.\n\nTwo notes for the reviewer, since the tier depends on them:\n\n- The example adds new executable content rather than correcting existing example text. It imports the kernel read-only, constructs no effects, and is not referenced by the package or by CI. If you would rather treat new executable example code as governed, say so and I will move it to the governed path.\n- The page restates product claims, so it was written to stay inside what the README already states. Where the code is weaker than a summary would suggest, the page says so — `posix-rlimit-only` is described as not a sandbox for adversarial code, and the Curator's `introduced-code-license-declared` checklist item is shown reporting `not-evaluated` with its recorded reason.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `python examples/sample-work-dag/build_sample_dag.py` — the graph validates; six ready waves; the eight malformed variants return the validator's messages (overlapping write scopes, cycle, self-dependency, depth mismatch, uncovered charter acceptance, fanout limit), and the overlapping pair is accepted once its dependency edge is restored.\n- `hive-mind demo` — run from a checkout; every ledger event, receipt field, digest, checklist finding, and budget figure quoted on the page comes from that bundle.\n- Page rendered in Chromium at 1440, 1320, 1024, 768, and 390 px in both colour schemes: no horizontal page overflow at any width, no console errors, and wide figures and code blocks scroll inside their own containers.\n\n**CI gate not green in this environment, and not because of this change.** `python -m unittest discover -s tests` reports the same 39 failures and 102 errors on this branch and on a clean tree with these files removed. The causes are environmental:\n\n- 134 of the 141 disappear when the container's `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT` / `GIT_CONFIG_KEY_*` proxy variables are unset. With them set, `demo_fixture._git` inherits a config count with no matching keys and git exits with `missing config key GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0`.\n- Of the 7 that remain, `test_generic_dag_v3_overlay` and `test_governance` need commit `9b1cbcfe` and tag `archive/evidence-corpus-2026-08-03`, neither of which exists in this clone (210 commits, 0 tags); `test_hive_cortex_explorer` and `test_pit_oracle` trip the Explorer's own `inherited Git environment injection is not allowed` rule against the same injected variables, which is the control working as intended.\n\nPlease confirm the gate on a clean checkout before merging.\n\n## Safety\n\n- [x] No credentials, private data, or unrelated generated artifacts are included.\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md).\n\n---\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Q1xG6bfCD2r5QVUGtq7AFi\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01Q1xG6bfCD2r5QVUGtq7AFi)_",
